[21.937883, "o", "\u001b[36m\u001b[1m> Bash\u001b[0m S=/tmp/claude-1000/-tmp-morning-plain/f4cc5982-8527-4585-9778-7f25f3413caf/scratchpad; mkdir -p\r\n     $S && cp /tmp/morning-plain/data/inventory.db $S/copy.db && cd /tmp/morning-plain && \r\n    ORDER_SYNC_LOG_LEVEL=INFO python3 job.py --source api --db $S/copy.db; echo exit=$?; \r\n    ORDER_SYNC_LOG_LEVEL=INFO python3 job.py --db $S/copy.db; echo exit=$?\r\n"]
[22.62925, "o", "\u001b[2m  2026-11-26 23:55:52,922 order_sync INFO fetching orders from http://127.0.0.1:38437\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  2026-11-26 23:55:53,432 order_sync INFO 9 orders read, 8 applied in this run\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  sync finished: OK\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  exit=0\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  2026-11-26 23:55:53,511 order_sync INFO reading orders from /tmp/morning-plain/data/orders.csv\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  2026-11-26 23:55:53,512 order_sync INFO 9 orders read, 0 applied in this run\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  sync finished: OK\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  exit=0\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n"]
[40.914429, "o", "\u001b[36m\u001b[1m> Bash\u001b[0m S=/tmp/claude-1000/-tmp-morning-plain/f4cc5982-8527-4585-9778-7f25f3413caf/scratchpad; cp\r\n    /tmp/morning-plain/data/inventory.db $S/copy2.db; printf \r\n    'order_id,placed_at,sku,quantity,channel,unit\\nX-1,2026-11-26T10:00,KBD-0101 \r\n    ,3,web,each\\nX-2,2026-11-26T10:01,KBD-0101,3,web,units\\n' > $S/bad.csv; cd /tmp/morning-plain; \r\n    echo \"-- default (cron) logging:\"; python3 job.py --db $S/copy2.db --orders $S/bad.csv; echo \r\n    exit=$?; echo \"-- with WARNING:\"; ORDER_SYNC_LOG_LEVEL=WARNING python3 job.py --db $S/copy2.db \r\n    --orders $S/bad.csv; echo exit=$?\r\n"]
[41.137582, "o", "\u001b[2m  -- default (cron) logging:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  sync finished: OK\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  exit=0\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  -- with WARNING:\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[41.137621, "o", "\u001b[2m  2026-11-26 23:56:12,018 order_sync WARNING order X-1 skipped: SKU 'KBD-0101 ' is not in the \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  format AAA-0000\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  2026-11-26 23:56:12,018 order_sync WARNING order X-2 skipped: unit 'units' is not one of each, \u001b[0m\r\n"]
[41.137635, "o", "\u001b[2m  pack\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  sync finished: OK\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[41.137759, "o", "\u001b[2m  exit=0\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n"]
[55.05979, "o", "Last night's job skipped some orders, and the code is built so that a skip leaves no message and \r\nstill ends in \"sync finished: OK\". The stock for those products was never reduced.\r\n\r\n**What the data shows**\r\n\r\n- Last night's batch was SO-20501…SO-20538. Five of those orders are missing from `applied_orders`:\r\n **SO-20521, SO-20526, SO-20530, SO-20533, SO-20537**. Every other order in the range was applied.\r\n- Compared with `data/count.csv`, three products show more stock in the system than on the shelf, \r\nwhich is what you get when sales were not taken off:\r\n\r\n  | SKU | System | Counted | Diff |\r\n  |---|---|---|---|\r\n  | KBD-0101 | 103 | 94 | +9 |\r\n  | HDS-0402 | 61 | 57 | +4 |\r\n  | MON-0201 | 38 | 37 | +1 |\r\n\r\n  The other five SKUs match the count exactly.\r\n- The current `data/orders.csv` (SO-20539…46) is the next batch, not last night's. On a copy of the\r\n DB, all 8 new orders apply cleanly, so this file isn't the problem.\r\n\r\n**Why it happened without any warning**\r\n\r\n1. `job.py:53-59`: `sync_orders` wraps each order in `except Exception`. It logs a warning and \r\nmoves on. Bad SKU format, bad unit, quantity out of range, unknown SKU and database errors are all \r\nhandled the same way.\r\n2. `job.py:30`: the default log level is `ERROR`, so those warnings are never printed. Cron \r\ncaptures nothing.\r\n3. `job.py:78-82`: the job prints \"sync finished: OK\" and exits 0 whatever the skip count was.\r\n4. `parse.py:38`: the SKU is the one field that isn't `.strip()`ed. A stray space (`\"KBD-0101 \"`) \r\nfails validation and the order is dropped.\r\n\r\nI tested this on a scratch copy of the DB with a SKU that has a trailing space and an unknown unit \r\n(`units`). Both orders were skipped, and with default logging the output was just `sync finished: \r\nOK`, exit 0. With `ORDER_SYNC_LOG_LEVEL=WARNING` the two skip messages appear.\r\n\r\n**What I couldn't find out**\r\n\r\nNothing on this machine records why each of the five orders was skipped. Only \"OK\" was logged, and \r\nthere's no `seed/` folder or `.ormaos/` recording here. The diffs add up to 14 items across 3 SKUs \r\nfrom 5 orders, so some of those orders may have been genuinely invalid and others real sales. Those\r\n details are only in the sales system. In production the job runs with `--source api`, and the API \r\nhands each order over once, so the job won't get these orders again on its own. You'll need to look\r\n up the five order IDs in the sales system and correct the stock by hand.\r\n\r\nAlso, `data/cron.log` shows two runs in the same second. That probably didn't cause this (an order \r\nalready applied is skipped, and the DB writes are transactional). But if two runs fetch from the \r\nreal API at once, the batch could be split between them, so it's worth checking why it ran twice.\r\n\r\n**Suggested fixes** (I haven't changed any code):\r\n- Count the skipped orders, log them at ERROR, and exit non-zero (or print \"OK with N skipped\") \r\nwhenever there are any.\r\n- Write rejected orders to a table such as `rejected_orders`, with the raw row and the reason. \r\nBecause the API can't resend orders, this is the only way to recover them later.\r\n- Strip the SKU in `parse.py`, as the other fields already are.\r\n- Catch only `ValidationError` and `UnknownSkuError` per order, so database errors stop the run \r\ninstead of skipping orders.\r\n\r\nI didn't change the real `data/inventory.db`. All test runs used copies in `/tmp/claude-1000/-tmp-\r\nmorning-plain/f4cc5982-8527-4585-9778-7f25f3413caf/scratchpad/`. Do you want me to make these \r\nfixes?\r\n\r\n"]
[58.018727, "o", "\u001b[2m-- end of the run: 5 tool calls, 57 s --\u001b[0m\r\n"]
